That movie from the 50s?

35
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:51,240
Oh, man.

36
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:53,360
Thanks again for listening once again, fellas.

37
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:54,800
Make sure you sing that song a lot.

38
00:01:54,940 --> 00:01:55,700
It's a great song.

39
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:59,540
Oh, well, this is podcast number 23.

40
00:01:59,540 --> 00:02:29,520
We'll be right back.

41
00:02:29,540 --> 00:02:59,519
We'll be right back.

42
00:03:00,140 --> 00:03:02,320
You'll find yourself in complete agreement.

43
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:05,680
Seek professional help as soon as possible.

44
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:10,660
I'm kidding. Thank you, Patrick.

45
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:12,160
Kind words.

46
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:14,799
In Scout Mastership, in seven minutes or less,

47
00:03:15,660 --> 00:03:18,940
we're going to talk about one of the tools in our toolbox,

48
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:22,620
one of the parts of the program, and that's the uniform.

49
00:03:22,620 --> 00:03:28,320
Then we're going to do the first of a three-part series

50
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:31,620
on starting your own high adventure program.

51
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:34,640
Yeah, that's right. You can do that, actually.

52
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:35,140
Yeah.

53
00:03:37,739 --> 00:03:40,839
I'll explain more later on in the podcast about it,

54
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:44,720
but no troops should go without being able to participate

55
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:46,559
in some kind of a high adventure activity.

56
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:49,160
once a year is good.

57
00:03:50,060 --> 00:03:51,480
Once every other year is fine.

58
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:57,240
If you can't afford to go out to one of the big high adventure bases

59
00:03:57,240 --> 00:04:02,060
or, you know, you can do something closer to home.

60
00:04:02,920 --> 00:04:05,580
You can do something that works in with your schedule a little bit better.

61
00:04:06,220 --> 00:04:09,180
And that's what we're going to be talking about over the next three podcasts.

62
00:04:09,280 --> 00:04:10,680
So part one of that will be coming up.

63
00:04:11,340 --> 00:04:13,000
Sounds like a pretty full slate, huh?

64
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:14,560
Well, let's get at it.

65
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:19,680
Scouts Mastership in seven minutes or less.

66
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:29,640
So you volunteer, you become a scout leader,

67
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:32,280
and you want to do things properly.

68
00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:34,320
You want to do things right.

69
00:04:34,920 --> 00:04:37,080
Now, maybe you're more detail-oriented than I am.

70
00:04:37,260 --> 00:04:38,540
I'm not a big detail guy.

71
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:42,020
Because there are rules and regulations for things in scouting,

72
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:44,040
sometimes we get a little bogged down in them.

73
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:46,680
And we forget about the success of our scouts.

74
00:04:47,380 --> 00:04:50,160
Now, when I say we, I mean we.

75
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:51,600
You and me.

76
00:04:52,060 --> 00:04:55,560
I have a tendency to get bogged down in little things sometimes.

77
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:57,220
So what we're about to talk about

78
00:04:57,220 --> 00:05:03,500
is not intended to try and tell you that you're wrong and I'm right.

79
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:07,140
It's just to say, let's examine exactly what goes on.

80
00:05:07,140 --> 00:05:10,700
Let's examine the policies and things like that.

81
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:12,180
And then let's move on.

82
00:05:12,719 --> 00:05:15,840
Because the success of our scouts is our single goal.

83
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:19,620
And we have a many-faceted program,

84
00:05:19,919 --> 00:05:21,840
one facet of which is the uniform.

85
00:05:22,539 --> 00:05:26,919
And there are a lot of urban legends and misunderstandings

86
00:05:26,919 --> 00:05:31,539
surrounding the wearing and application of the scout uniform.

87
00:05:31,540 --> 00:05:36,360
And I think it would be helpful to kind of clear things up.

88
00:05:37,980 --> 00:05:40,480
Here's what the founder, Baden Powell,

89
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:46,000
had to say about scout uniforms in his little book on scout mastership.

90
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,420
He said, I've often said, I don't care a fig whether a scout wears a uniform or not

91
00:05:51,420 --> 00:05:54,760
so long as his heart is in his work and he carries out the scout law.

92
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:59,840
So Baden Powell's attitude when he was putting all this together was,

93
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:05,420
what's really important is that guys are doing scouting.

94
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:09,040
But the fact is, he goes on to say,

95
00:06:09,340 --> 00:06:11,360
is there's hardly a scout who does not wear a uniform.

96
00:06:11,540 --> 00:06:14,280
If he can afford to buy it, the spirit prompts him to it.

97
00:06:14,280 --> 00:06:18,179
The same rule applies naturally to those who carry on the scouting movement,

98
00:06:18,340 --> 00:06:19,780
the scout masters and commissioners.

99
00:06:19,859 --> 00:06:22,919
There's no obligation on them to wear a uniform if they don't like it.

100
00:06:24,260 --> 00:06:26,940
At the same time, they have in their positions

101
00:06:26,940 --> 00:06:29,479
to think of others rather than of themselves.

102
00:06:30,179 --> 00:06:33,840
Personally, I put on a uniform even if I only have a patrol to inspect

103
00:06:33,840 --> 00:06:36,580
because I am certain that it raises the moral tone of the boys.

104
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:38,960
It heightens their estimation of the uniform

105
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:41,940
when they see that it is not beneath a grown man to wear it.

106
00:06:41,940 --> 00:06:46,660
It heightens their estimation of themselves when they find themselves taken seriously by men

107
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:50,660
who also count it of importance to be in the same brotherhood with them.

108
00:06:51,780 --> 00:06:54,600
So there's an answer to one question.

109
00:06:54,760 --> 00:06:55,940
Must a scout wear a uniform?

110
00:06:56,840 --> 00:06:59,760
And Baden Powell himself said, well, not really.

111
00:07:00,260 --> 00:07:03,600
No, hopefully we'll be inspired to wear uniforms as scouts.

112
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:06,940
A Cub Scout is very interested in the uniform, isn't he?

113
00:07:06,940 --> 00:07:09,820
He's very anxious to get one and then as they get a little bit older,

114
00:07:10,020 --> 00:07:12,040
they're like, meh, I'm not really sure about this.

115
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:15,900
You know, they're not really sure about anything at a certain age.

116
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:20,680
And then they kind of make an uncomfortable piece with the uniform

117
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:23,640
and they will wear it, sometimes begrudgingly.

118
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:25,520
And then as they get a little bit older than that,

119
00:07:26,180 --> 00:07:30,220
you know, then they really appreciate the uniform and they're very proud of it.

120
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:34,480
That's kind of the cycle that most of the scouts that I've ever worked with go through.

121
00:07:34,920 --> 00:07:36,580
A scout doesn't have to wear a uniform,

122
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:40,240
but hopefully the spirit of the movement and the encouragement of his peers

123
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:45,640
and his leaders will cause him to understand why we wear a uniform.

124
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:48,580
One of the reasons is personal equality.

125
00:07:49,520 --> 00:07:52,920
Uniform represents a very democratic idea of equality

126
00:07:52,920 --> 00:07:57,400
and it will bring people from all different racial, economic, and religious,

127
00:07:57,600 --> 00:08:01,420
and national, and ethnic, and political, and geographical backgrounds

128
00:08:01,420 --> 00:08:03,880
together in the scouting tradition.

129
00:08:05,640 --> 00:08:06,820
One is identification.

130
00:08:07,280 --> 00:08:08,620
Hey, there's a scout. How do you know?

131
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:09,380
Wearing a uniform.

132
00:08:09,760 --> 00:08:10,300
Oh, right.

133
00:08:12,560 --> 00:08:17,380
One is achievement because you can demonstrate your achievement on the uniform.

134
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:19,380
And the other is personal commitment.

135
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:21,440
Hey, I'm committed to this.

136
00:08:21,440 --> 00:08:22,420
I'm wearing this uniform.

137
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:27,120
I was going to say I'm wearing this goofy uniform, but it's not.

138
00:08:27,260 --> 00:08:28,620
Yeah. Okay. All right.

139
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:31,220
It's not goofy. All right.

140
00:08:32,500 --> 00:08:36,240
So how do we get scouts to wear uniforms?

141
00:08:36,380 --> 00:08:38,360
Well, we encourage them to do that.

142
00:08:38,360 --> 00:08:40,920
We don't castigate them for wearing an improper uniform.

143
00:08:41,220 --> 00:08:42,400
Here's an example.

144
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:45,240
I have a scout, brand new scout.

145
00:08:45,460 --> 00:08:48,960
He joined the troop and then he was very anxious to get a uniform

146
00:08:48,960 --> 00:08:50,940
because he wanted to be like all the other guys.

147
00:08:50,940 --> 00:08:53,740
So the boy shows up and he's got a couple of patches misplaced.

148
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:54,880
Now, I had two choices.

149
00:08:55,080 --> 00:09:00,820
First choice was to go up to the boy and say, hey, you got your uniform patches on all wrong

150
00:09:00,820 --> 00:09:06,760
and make a big deal over it and kind of embarrass him or make him feel bad.

151
00:09:06,760 --> 00:09:13,840
Or I could basically kind of look in the other direction and say, wow, you know, you look great in that uniform.

152
00:09:14,380 --> 00:09:15,319
I'm glad you got it.

153
00:09:15,340 --> 00:09:17,080
And it's great to have you as a member of the troop.

154
00:09:17,080 --> 00:09:26,800
And then I will have a quiet word with his patrol leader or our senior patrol leader and say, look, I don't want you to embarrass or castigate the scout,

155
00:09:26,980 --> 00:09:31,800
but, you know, he's a little confused about some of the patches and things like that.

156
00:09:31,860 --> 00:09:33,280
So why don't you help him out?

157
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:37,580
And we'll get a needle and thread and you guys can take care of it.

158
00:09:37,580 --> 00:09:49,580
So as in all things, we're encouraging, coaching, we're being the good guys and we're helping instead of trying to be a drill sergeant

159
00:09:49,580 --> 00:09:55,880
and look at, you know, the state of the brass on the uniform and have they got everything just right and we'll get out a ruler to measure it.

160
00:09:55,900 --> 00:09:57,440
Hey, take it easy.

161
00:09:58,620 --> 00:09:59,000
All right.

162
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,420
How about this question?

163
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:02,980
What's a class A uniform?

164
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:04,980
What's a class B uniform?

165
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:08,740
I have even heard mentioned the rare class C uniform.

166
00:10:09,660 --> 00:10:10,820
What is all that about?

167
00:10:11,500 --> 00:10:12,540
Well, they don't exist.

168
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:14,840
There's one uniform and that's it.

169
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:18,720
You know, and it's described in the insignia guide.

170
00:10:19,080 --> 00:10:20,320
And that's it.

171
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:22,660
Now, well, wait a minute.

172
00:10:23,020 --> 00:10:25,500
That doesn't sound right, does it?

173
00:10:25,520 --> 00:10:27,160
Because everybody talks about this.

174
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:29,699
Everybody talks about, hey, wear your class A uniform.

175
00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:31,240
Hey, wear your class B uniform.

176
00:10:31,860 --> 00:10:36,740
Well, however that got started, you won't find them in scouting literature anywhere.

177
00:10:37,699 --> 00:10:40,219
Not in official BSA scouting literature.

178
00:10:41,199 --> 00:10:47,760
Now, it might be useful sometimes for guys to wear like a troop t-shirt and their scout shorts and socks and just as an identifier.

179
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:49,120
And it might be a little more casual.

180
00:10:49,520 --> 00:10:51,439
And if you want to call it class B, that's fine with me.

181
00:10:51,480 --> 00:10:55,079
And if you want to call wearing the shirt and the hat and the sash and all that business class A,

182
00:10:55,080 --> 00:10:57,780
it doesn't really matter to me.

183
00:10:58,080 --> 00:11:02,060
We seem to spin our wheels a lot of times over insignificant details.

184
00:11:02,060 --> 00:11:06,460
And we miss the big picture, which is the success of our scouts.

185
00:11:06,700 --> 00:11:11,660
I've made an attempt here just to talk about uniforming in a very realistic way.

186
00:11:11,780 --> 00:11:14,500
And I've hit on, I know, what are our few hot button items.

187
00:11:14,780 --> 00:11:17,000
As always, we focus on the success of our scouts.

188
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:24,640
And we use the methods of scouting, like uniforming, not to castigate or to browbeat our scouts, but to encourage them.

189
00:11:25,480 --> 00:11:25,700
Right?

190
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:29,600
And that's what's really, really important.

191
00:11:29,600 --> 00:11:58,740
Over the next several podcasts, we're going to be talking about high adventure and high adventure trips.

192
00:11:58,740 --> 00:12:04,280
Now, I should start out by saying there's really no, like, set definition of what a high adventure trip is.

193
00:12:04,320 --> 00:12:12,980
I mean, there's no minimum number of days or minimum number of crew members or, you know, minimum number of miles that you have to cover or anything like that.

194
00:12:13,080 --> 00:12:18,640
What this is, is it's a trip that is aimed at more senior scouts.

195
00:12:20,140 --> 00:12:24,040
Most of the high adventure bases now require scouts to be at least 13.

196
00:12:24,040 --> 00:12:33,579
And it is a multi-day experience, maybe backpacking or canoeing or on a sailboat or something like that.

197
00:12:34,040 --> 00:12:38,839
So don't get tied up in there is a specific definition of a high adventure trip.

198
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:42,660
But I'm just going to use that term to identify what we're talking about.

199
00:12:42,839 --> 00:12:47,079
I might talk about crews and I might talk about troops here.

200
00:12:47,080 --> 00:12:54,280
And, yes, there are some official definitions of what a Boy Scout troop and a venture crew is and what venturers are.

201
00:12:54,720 --> 00:13:04,080
I know that within our way of managing high adventure trips, when we get the people who are interested in the trip signed on, we form crews.

202
00:13:04,080 --> 00:13:11,440
So I'm going to be using the word crew here within the context of what we do as a scout troop.

203
00:13:11,740 --> 00:13:22,900
But don't confuse it with the fact that you have to have a venture crew to be able to participate in high adventure or to have, you know, to have a high adventure crew.

204
00:13:24,480 --> 00:13:25,900
That's not necessarily so.

205
00:13:25,960 --> 00:13:28,780
It's something that you can create within a troop.

206
00:13:28,780 --> 00:13:34,900
Now, that doesn't mean that you're going to be able to get the insignia and the uniforming of a venture crew.

207
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:36,920
But I think you're understanding what I'm saying.

208
00:13:37,699 --> 00:13:41,360
And I don't want to confuse you with these terms as we talk about the subject.

209
00:13:42,579 --> 00:13:47,480
Thousands of scouts participate and enjoy the big three high adventure bases each year.

210
00:13:47,540 --> 00:13:52,160
I'm talking about Philmont in New Mexico where there's backpacking.

211
00:13:52,760 --> 00:13:56,500
Our sea base in Florida where you're on a sailboat.

212
00:13:56,500 --> 00:14:02,700
And Northern Tier in Eli, Minnesota where it's canoeing.

213
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:08,100
These are the three most familiar high adventure bases.

214
00:14:09,100 --> 00:14:12,580
And there are literally dozens of other council-based programs.

215
00:14:12,700 --> 00:14:15,080
They might be backpacking or canoeing or something like that.

216
00:14:15,140 --> 00:14:17,520
But there's a good many of them out there.

217
00:14:18,440 --> 00:14:21,560
I have colleagues in scouting who recommend these things highly.

218
00:14:21,560 --> 00:14:28,020
And I want to talk about more of the other end of the spectrum in this series.

219
00:14:28,319 --> 00:14:33,719
And that are troop and crew-based programs that explore a little bit less known territory.

220
00:14:34,219 --> 00:14:35,500
I will tell you this.

221
00:14:35,579 --> 00:14:41,339
I have led high adventure type experiences inside and outside of scouting.

222
00:14:41,479 --> 00:14:43,619
I've led and participated in a good many of them.

223
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:45,079
They might be backpacking.

224
00:14:45,119 --> 00:14:45,920
They might be climbing.

225
00:14:45,920 --> 00:14:48,520
They might be canoeing or something like that.

226
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,599
But they all have one thing in common.

227
00:14:51,740 --> 00:14:52,300
And this is it.

228
00:14:52,400 --> 00:15:00,819
The people who go and their level of preparation is always more important than the place that you go.

229
00:15:00,820 --> 00:15:06,600
So if you'll just lay aside the idea that you have to spend thousands of dollars to travel

230
00:15:06,600 --> 00:15:10,700
and thousands of dollars on equipment and things like that.

231
00:15:11,500 --> 00:15:14,520
And just think about the fact that you're going to take a group

232
00:15:14,520 --> 00:15:17,380
and you're going to have a certain set of goals and challenges.

233
00:15:18,100 --> 00:15:20,980
And you're going to go on this multi-day high adventure thing.

234
00:15:21,420 --> 00:15:23,900
That is what I really want you to center on.

235
00:15:23,900 --> 00:15:32,120
Because preparing your group to go, your crew to go, is the most important part of the entire thing.

236
00:15:32,880 --> 00:15:34,240
You know, here's an example.

237
00:15:35,180 --> 00:15:37,920
You're going to go to Philmont, the crown jewel of scout camps.

238
00:15:38,520 --> 00:15:41,240
And the skies are going to stay blue the whole time you're there.

239
00:15:41,319 --> 00:15:42,160
It's not going to rain.

240
00:15:42,699 --> 00:15:44,280
And the temperatures are going to be just right.

241
00:15:44,319 --> 00:15:46,000
It's going to be nice and warm during the day.

242
00:15:46,079 --> 00:15:47,400
It's going to be nice and cool at night.

243
00:15:47,459 --> 00:15:48,720
There's not going to be any bugs.

244
00:15:49,140 --> 00:15:50,760
You're going to have five-star meals.

245
00:15:50,760 --> 00:15:55,580
And it's just, you know, it looks like it's going to be a wonderful trip.

246
00:15:55,700 --> 00:16:00,040
Well, if you're not prepared properly and you go there with a dysfunctional crew,

247
00:16:00,260 --> 00:16:03,360
it's going to be like the Bataan Death March.

248
00:16:03,480 --> 00:16:04,460
I guarantee you.

249
00:16:05,300 --> 00:16:09,720
Likewise, if you spend a few days hiking in a state park somewhere,

250
00:16:10,540 --> 00:16:14,100
you know, maybe less inspiring an environment,

251
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:17,460
and it rains every single day,

252
00:16:17,460 --> 00:16:21,300
and you're eating oatmeal and ramen,

253
00:16:21,880 --> 00:16:24,980
if you have a sharp crew who knows their stuff

254
00:16:24,980 --> 00:16:27,320
and who's able to get along together,

255
00:16:28,100 --> 00:16:30,320
it's a lifelong golden memory.

256
00:16:33,620 --> 00:16:35,880
Yeah, it is that important.

257
00:16:37,040 --> 00:16:41,220
High adventure should take a crew to a new level of experience in the outdoors

258
00:16:41,220 --> 00:16:43,480
and in developing those things that are the aim of scouting,

259
00:16:43,680 --> 00:16:48,060
the skills and the competencies that we want to develop as scouting.

260
00:16:48,600 --> 00:16:52,940
Growing confidence in their abilities and their ability to lead

261
00:16:52,940 --> 00:16:55,740
and their opportunity to follow

262
00:16:55,740 --> 00:16:58,580
and their appreciation for the abilities of others

263
00:16:58,580 --> 00:17:01,000
are all part of a successful high adventure trip.

264
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:04,120
These trips don't have to be ambitious.

265
00:17:04,540 --> 00:17:05,680
They don't have to be expensive.

266
00:17:06,560 --> 00:17:08,079
They don't have to be far flung.

267
00:17:08,079 --> 00:17:10,520
I mean, they can be on the other side of the world

268
00:17:10,520 --> 00:17:11,720
or the other side of the state

269
00:17:11,720 --> 00:17:13,060
or an hour from your house.

270
00:17:13,659 --> 00:17:15,540
What really matters is the crew

271
00:17:15,540 --> 00:17:17,419
and the way it prepared for the trip.

272
00:17:18,500 --> 00:17:20,300
You know, good weather and good food

273
00:17:20,300 --> 00:17:22,099
and good surroundings are part of the picture,

274
00:17:22,220 --> 00:17:23,560
but the biggest part of the challenge

275
00:17:23,560 --> 00:17:27,899
and the satisfaction of accomplishing the goals

276
00:17:27,899 --> 00:17:30,399
is setting some good goals

277
00:17:30,399 --> 00:17:33,320
and knowing their importance to the trip

278
00:17:33,320 --> 00:17:34,600
and building on them

279
00:17:34,600 --> 00:17:37,159
by thoroughly preparing the crew to accomplish them.

280
00:17:37,160 --> 00:17:39,760
Now, here's a couple of things

281
00:17:39,760 --> 00:17:41,200
that figure into your rationale

282
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:43,200
of building a high adventure program

283
00:17:43,200 --> 00:17:45,920
that's based in your troop or your crew.

284
00:17:46,860 --> 00:17:50,580
It's based on a few logistic and philosophic criteria.

285
00:17:50,780 --> 00:17:54,520
One of the most important considerations is time and money.

286
00:17:55,580 --> 00:17:57,500
Yeah, I know, it's not that romantic,

287
00:17:57,760 --> 00:18:01,780
but it is going to come down to time and money.

288
00:18:01,780 --> 00:18:04,639
A lot of high adventure trips involve,

289
00:18:05,180 --> 00:18:09,300
for us, where we live in the mid-Atlantic,

290
00:18:09,980 --> 00:18:15,940
involve air travel and ground fees when we get there.

291
00:18:17,580 --> 00:18:20,399
And the cost for participating in one of those

292
00:18:20,399 --> 00:18:24,920
is going to be well over $1,000 for us.

293
00:18:24,920 --> 00:18:29,200
It also requires 12 or 14 days, including travel time.

294
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:33,940
Now, in comparison, the trips that we've put together as a troop

295
00:18:33,940 --> 00:18:38,300
cost about half as much, usually about $500 to $600.

296
00:18:39,080 --> 00:18:43,340
And they run from early Friday to the following Saturday night,

297
00:18:43,440 --> 00:18:46,020
and they only consume six vacation days.

298
00:18:47,120 --> 00:18:49,140
Now, this is a really important consideration

299
00:18:49,140 --> 00:18:51,920
because you're going to need adult leadership

300
00:18:51,920 --> 00:18:54,380
to participate in these trips.

301
00:18:55,120 --> 00:18:57,520
And a lot of people aren't going to be able to take

302
00:18:57,520 --> 00:19:01,000
the two-week vacation that is demanded

303
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:02,680
of some of these more ambitious trips.

304
00:19:04,260 --> 00:19:05,560
The other thing is,

305
00:19:06,200 --> 00:19:08,360
here's an interesting way of thinking about

306
00:19:08,360 --> 00:19:09,820
your high adventure plans.

307
00:19:09,860 --> 00:19:11,200
You want to ride a roller coaster

308
00:19:11,200 --> 00:19:13,220
or you want to pedal a bike.

309
00:19:14,820 --> 00:19:15,980
What do I mean by that?

310
00:19:15,980 --> 00:19:19,820
Well, a lot of the national programs

311
00:19:19,820 --> 00:19:22,140
are pre-planned, pre-set programs,

312
00:19:22,340 --> 00:19:24,440
and they involve a minimal amount of flexibility.

313
00:19:25,240 --> 00:19:26,740
The dates and the activities,

314
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:28,020
the equipment and the routes

315
00:19:28,020 --> 00:19:29,760
are pretty well predetermined.

316
00:19:30,400 --> 00:19:32,220
There's also a good deal of competition

317
00:19:32,220 --> 00:19:34,100
for the best routes and times.

318
00:19:34,820 --> 00:19:38,500
And that I liken to lining up for a roller coaster ride.

319
00:19:38,600 --> 00:19:40,560
I mean, you buy your ticket and you get in line.

320
00:19:42,560 --> 00:19:44,840
Troop-based or crew-based high adventure trips

321
00:19:44,840 --> 00:19:46,040
are a lot more flexible.

322
00:19:46,560 --> 00:19:48,060
And they're more like getting on your bike

323
00:19:48,060 --> 00:19:49,280
and heading out for a ride.

324
00:19:50,120 --> 00:19:52,419
Not that the roller coaster isn't fun and challenging,

325
00:19:52,520 --> 00:19:53,679
it's just a different approach.

326
00:19:53,760 --> 00:19:55,840
I'm not saying that one is better than the other,

327
00:19:55,959 --> 00:19:58,459
but this is the rationale that we went through

328
00:19:58,459 --> 00:20:00,240
when we were putting our high adventure

329
00:20:00,240 --> 00:20:01,520
troop program together.

330
00:20:03,199 --> 00:20:04,780
What does it take to put a troop

331
00:20:04,780 --> 00:20:06,340
high adventure program together?

332
00:20:07,120 --> 00:20:09,120
Well, it's not as difficult as you might think,

333
00:20:09,120 --> 00:20:10,879
and we'll begin next time

334
